<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oberto Doria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doria-family">Doria family</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oberto Doria was a Genoese admiral and statesman of the thirteenth century. From 1270 he shared power with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oberto-spinola">Oberto Spinola</a> as Captain of the People, a joint rule by the two great Ghibelline families that kept the Guelph nobles out of office. In 1284 he commanded the Genoese fleet that destroyed the Pisan navy at the Battle of Meloria, ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>'s maritime power.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Oberto Doria was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> before 1230, the oldest of four sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-doria">Pietro Doria</a> and Mabilia Casiccia. His brothers included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamba-doria">Lamba Doria</a>, who later defeated the Venetians in the War of Curzola, and the chronicler Iacopo Doria. His father kept out of the city's factional politics and built a flourishing trading company with his partner Poncio Riccio.</p><p>Oberto worked as his father's assistant before trading on his own account; from about 1250 surviving documents show him dealing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. His family's standing, with father and grandfather on the Great Council and the podestà living in the Doria palace, brought him early into public life: he is recorded as consiliator of the Commune at treaties in 1252 and 1256 and at an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1254.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the Genoese fleet under Lanfranco Borbonino was defeated and captured by the Venetians at the Battle of Trapani, Oberto received his first naval command, a fleet of 25 ships. According to the Annali Genovesi he owed it to his popularity with the merchants, who were dissatisfied with the Guelph faction. He sailed east, took Venetian merchant ships and sacked Canea on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, a Venetian colony, then avoided battle with Marco Zeno's thirty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a> and brought his booty home by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>.</p><p>On 28 October 1270 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oberto-spinola">Oberto Spinola</a> began a series of two-man governments of their families, holding dictatorial powers as captains of the people. He defended the republic against the Fieschi, who sought a lordship of their own in the Levante Ligure, storming Nicolò Fieschi's capital La Spezia in 1273, while his brother Jacopo fought the Grimaldi in the Ponente. Towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, he followed a cautious policy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the Battle of Meloria on 6 August 1284 he commanded the fleet together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedetto-zaccaria">Benedetto Zaccaria</a> and Corrado Spinola, son of his fellow captain. The victory let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> regain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. In 1285 he gave up the joint captaincy of his own accord but stayed in politics, and the peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> was signed in his house on 15 April 1288. He died shortly before 6 September 1306. His fifteen years of shared rule have been called the golden age of medieval Genoa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oberto Doria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its long rivalry with Pisa over Corsica, Sardinia and trade ended at the Battle of Meloria in 1284, when <b>Oberto Doria</b> destroyed the Pisan fleet and took thousands of prisoners, crippling Pisa as a naval power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Genoa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Genoa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1264" aria-label="Genoa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4764 593 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Oberto Doria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5214 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5214 863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1264">What was here in 1264? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
